live now to derry where the funeral of  the former sdlp leader and  nobel peace prize winner john hume has  just started this is john hoon  jr talking now and the former sdlp  leader  died on monday at the age of 83  following  a long illness um let's speak to our  senior island correspondent  david blevins um who's in london larry  um david good morning to you please  could you just give us an idea of the  significance  of this day  well it seems very unusual that we're  witnessing a  socially distanced funeral for the man  who spent his life  bringing people closer together but his  family did ask  the community not to line the streets to  light a candle at home to protect public  health  and in the last few minutes we've heard  tributes to john hume  read by the bishop of derry from pope  francis  and from the dalai lama and now we're  hearing a very personal tribute  from the family read by his son john jr  on father's day a few weeks ago as we  couldn't be with him  all his grandchildren posted him his  favorite chocolate bars  i hear they had a lovely feast and one  more  if god were here today in the fullness  of his health witnessing the current  tensions in the world  he wouldn't waste the opportunity to say  a few words himself  he'd talk about our common humanity the  need to respect diversity  and difference to protect and deepen  democracy  to value education and to place  non-violence  at the core of everything he might also  stress the need for a living wage  a roof over your head the right to  decent healthcare  and to education which was very close to  his heart  if we were if he were here now he might  quote us to his friend  congressman john lewis who sadly passed  away a few weeks ago  appealing to the goodness of every every  human being  and never giving up  dad was a dairy man to his core and  those deep roots of neighborhood  and community served to nourish him  throughout the difficult years  from the beginning the european union  was like a homecoming  bringing together diverse cultures in an  interdependent relationship  allowing for unique identities but at  the same time  holding the bigger picture of unified  kinship  at this time of planetary fragility  more than ever he would be urging that  we move beyond  our flag-based identities and recognize  that we need to protect  our common home central to dad's work  was a deep appreciation of human  interdependency  we all need one another we all have a  role to play  all our roles are of equal importance  in the last years of dad's life his  physical and mental health  became more and more visibly vulnerable  and yet in these those recent years more  than ever we as a family  witnessed the absolute importance of  dad's core ethos  of building community based on respect  and love  the kindness shown to dad by the people  of derry and donegal  they stopped to talk to him in the  street every day  guided him to protect his independence  when he was confused  and received him with gentleness when he  was agitated  was to provide a profound gift to all of  us  we are eternally grateful as a family to  all those that helped him over the years  in the last two years when he lost his  mobility and his sight  he moved to owen more nursing in this  his last home and it was a home a home  for all of us  all of us as a family were made very  welcome and became part of a new  community  of families and carers the deep  attention and love  shown to dad and to the many friends he  made amongst carers  residents and their families will remain  a lesson to us all  for the rest of our lives during the  long weeks of lockdown  when we as a family were unable to be  with them we knew  that despite the major difficulties of  infection management and bereavement  the care and nursing staff in unit one  were doing all their absolute best  to care deeply for him and for all his  fellow residents  we know that he continued to sing songs  every day to teach them all a wee bit of  french  to tell jokes to demand more bones  and to question everyone daily about  where they came from their origins and  their families  although he remembered nothing dad  remained deeply interested  in every individual he met right down to  the end of his death  dad was also a father a husband  and a man who loved and cared for his  family at all times  her mum was without a doubt dad's  greatest achievement  and she enabled him to reach his full  potential  mom and dad met at a dance and  borderland  the ballroom of romance just across the  border  in donegal the starting point for many a  dairy family  romance was followed by a wedding and a  december honeymoon  in a freezing bnb in gardner street in  dublin  for mum the quality of the accommodation  got better  as the marriage went up our mum who  loved supported and guided him  throughout his tireless work and later  in his frailty  was his greatest blessing none of us  remember him  changing nappies or indeed putting many  indeed any dinners on the table what we  do remember  are endless coffee cups and overflowing  ashtrays  newspapers everywhere and constant  stream of callers night and day  to our home and west empire but he was  there for all of us throughout our life  there were times when i think we all  felt that he was absent  but he wasn't he was just with us from  somewhere else  along with mum he taught us all our  values  and gave us all our moral comments and  for that  we will be forever in their debt  as a family we'll remember the man who  was rooted in this community  a man who was most comfortable sitting  in front of the tv  with half a dozen crunchy bars to keep  him company and his families around him  or the old-time holding court around the  corner in the park  a man who ordered the same dinner in the  same restaurants  in strasbourg and greencastle for 25  years  i'm sure he's up there now ordering his  creme brulee and off that awful sweet  wine that he loved  a man who loved darius is best be that  promoting the candy stripes across the  world  or the many choirs that he brought from  the city to europe to sing in brussels  and in strasbourg a man who himself  didn't need to be invited twice to lift  the mic  and give us a blast at the time i loved  so well our math highlands  and many many many more  a man who truly believed to derry and  the talents of our people  and became our greatest ambassador to  the world  a man who loved only god and spent most  of that time time  in bombay and green castle in movaut  where he was finally able to switch off  and relax  and get that peace and quiet that he  richly needed and deserved  the care god received in the last years  of his life  allowed him to retain his dignity  individuality  and his magnificent strength of  character despite his overwhelming  and growing disabilities it allowed him  to overcome  if you were here he would urge us all to  look at those young carers  and the incredible and heroic daily work  they do  as a model for future leadership their  ethos of deep respect  i respect for everyone regardless of  where they come from  our stage of life these are the  foundation stones  that are critical to all communities the  reverend martin luther king  might describe it as the politics of  love  dad would urge us to listen so that in  spite of it all  we shall overcome thank you dad  okay for a life well  a very personal tribute from john hume  jr speaking about his father's focus on  common humanity the need to respect  diversity and difference  to protect and deepen democracy and to  place  non-violence at the absolute center of  everything  also paying tribute of course to his  mother  pat who journeyed with john hume  the whole way through his lengthy  political career  they will be disappointed that this  cathedral cannot  be filled today but they are  honored by the presence of the irish  president the taoiseach prime minister  michael martin  and by the presence of the first and  deputy first ministers of northern  ireland arlene foster and michelle  o'neill the women who are of course  sharing the power john hume envisaged in  the devolved government  of northern ireland the women tasked  with carrying on the  task he started of building peace and  defending it  in northern ireland  john hume one of the key architects of  the northern ireland peace process the  former sdlp  leader being laid to rest today in derry  after his death last monday aged 83  following a long illness
 

